==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|directif}}. The noun senses are from {{bor|en|fr|directive}} (feminine form of the adjective).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/daɪˈɹɛk.tɪv/|/dɪˈɹɛk.tɪv/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-directive.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/dəˈɹɛk.tɪv/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛktɪv|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# That which [[direct]]s; serving to [[direct]], [[indicate]], or [[guide]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=Colin Gray|title=Enterprise and Culture|page=54
|passage=A '''directive''' management style is stronger among owners with 'lifestyle' as a business objective than among those with business/economic objectives.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Relating to the [[directive case]].

